window.AG_CHAPTERS["17"] = {"id":"17","title":"The Thread She Cannot See","titleZh":"看不見的線","patient":{"name":"Su-shi 蘇氏","sprite":"girl"},"scene":{"img":"art/scene-treatment.jpg","key":"rain"},"pattern":"Heart and Liver Blood Deficiency 心肝血虛","principle":"nourish blood, settle the spirit, and let the eyes and sinews be fed","tongue":"pale, small and slightly dry, with a thin white coating","pulseNote":{"en":"Fine as a silk thread under your finger — there, but you must not press hard or you lose it.","zh":"細如絲線應指，按之稍重則失"},"needleAsk":{"en":"Needle <b>Sh&#233;nm&#233;n HT7</b>. Tap its exact location.","zh":"行針 — 點出穴位精確位置"},"target":{"id":"HT7","zh":"神門","py":"Sh&#233;nm&#233;n","x":236,"y":656,"img":"art/body-forearm-palmar.jpg","w":434,"h":1159,"view":{"x":92,"y":520,"w":212,"h":255},"decoys":[{"x":140,"y":652},{"x":201,"y":570},{"x":236,"y":604},{"x":210,"y":656},{"x":258,"y":690}],"note":"<b>神門 Sh&#233;nm&#233;n HT7</b> — at the ulnar end of the wrist crease, on the radial side of the flexor carpi ulnaris tendon, in the depression at the proximal border of the pisiform. Source point and Stream point of the Heart. It calms the spirit, settles palpitations, and is the first point for insomnia, poor memory and anxiety.<br>腕橫紋尺側端，尺側腕屈肌腱橈側，豌豆骨上緣凹陷中。心之原穴、輸穴。安神定悸，主失眠、健忘、驚悸。"},"hints":{"ask":{"en":"Divide her complaints into what her <b>mind</b> is doing and what her <b>body</b> is doing. Two lists.","zh":"分其症為<b>神志</b>之見與<b>形體</b>之見。當作二列。"},"look":{"en":"A small pale tongue is a question about quantity, not about temperature.","zh":"舌淡而小者，所問在量，不在寒熱。"},"pulse":{"en":"One quality, and it means the vessel is not full. Ask yourself what fills a vessel.","zh":"脈見一象，主脈道不充。當自問：充脈道者何物？"},"dx":{"en":"Name what is short. Then give each organ its own evidence. Then notice what is <b>absent</b>.","zh":"先名其所虧，次為二臟各立其據，末察其所<b>無</b>。"},"rx":{"en":"One point for the spirit, one where blood is made, one where three yin channels meet.","zh":"一穴以安神，一穴以生血，一穴在三陰交會之處。"},"tech":{"en":"She is empty, and she is fragile. The second word changes how you handle the needle.","zh":"其人虛，且弱。後一字，正所以定持針之法。"}},"reactions":[{"who":"master","en":"Master Shen moves the lamp further away before he begins.","zh":"參師父先把燈挪遠了些，然後才開始。"},{"who":"patient","en":"Su-shi rubs the back of her hand as if it had gone to sleep.","zh":"蘇氏搓著手背，像那隻手麻了。"},{"who":"boy","en":"The herb boy sets the stool nearer the door, out of habit. Master Shen moves it back.","zh":"藥童習慣性地把凳子挪近門口。參師父又挪了回來。"},{"who":"master","en":"\"She lost a great deal of blood a year ago. That is history, not diagnosis. Examine her today.\"","zh":"「她一年前失血甚多。此為病史，非診斷。當診其今日。」"},{"who":"patient","en":"\"I keep forgetting what I came into a room for,\" she says, and looks embarrassed.","zh":"「我常常走進一間屋子，就忘了要做什麼，」她說，神色有些窘。"},{"who":"master","en":"\"Forgetting, and dry eyes, and numb hands. Ask yourself what one substance feeds all three.\"","zh":"「健忘、目乾、手麻。當自問：一物而能養此三者，何物也？」"}],"remedy":{"ask":"excess_def","look":"excess_def","pulse":"excess_def","dx":"excess_def","rx":"channel_organ","tech":"excess_def"},"reward":{"cardEn":"Blood Houses the Spirit","cardZh":"血養神 · 心肝同治","cardTxt":"Blood does two jobs that look unrelated until you see them together. The Heart stores the spirit, and blood is the spirit&#39;s house — short of blood, the spirit has nowhere to settle, so there are palpitations, poor sleep, dream-disturbed nights and forgetting. The Liver stores blood and sends it out to the eyes, the sinews and the nails — short of blood, vision blurs, hands go numb, calves cramp, nails crack and periods thin out. Mind symptoms and body symptoms, one substance. And notice what is missing: no night sweats, no burning palms, no red peeled tongue. Blood deficiency without empty heat is not yin deficiency.","herbId":"hongzao","herbEn":"Jujube","herbZh":"紅棗","herbImg":"art/card-hongzao.jpg","sprite":"art/sprite-renshen-jump.webp","herbTxt":"Sweet and warm, entering Spleen and Stomach. It supplements the middle so that blood can be made in the first place, and it settles the spirit — which is why it appears in the classical formula for a woman who weeps without knowing why. In food therapy it is the everyday version of this chapter: a few dates simmered with longan and a little ginger. Sweet and cloying in quantity, so it is measured, not heaped."},"endings":{"three":["Six weeks on, Su-shi is embroidering again by daylight. \"I still stop at dusk,\" she says, \"but I know why now.\"<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You kept the two organs together instead of choosing between them, and you noticed there was no heat. Most students see dry eyes and reach for yin.\"","六週後，蘇氏白日又能作繡活了。「入了黃昏我還是得停，」她說，「但如今我知道是為什麼。」<br><b>參師父：</b>「你不於二臟之間取捨，而合治之；且察其無熱。多數學生見目乾即思陰虛。」"],"two":["The palpitations have settled. Her hands still go numb by evening.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"You treated the Heart well. Something else is not being fed — go and find out what the Liver sends blood to.\"","悸已定。入暮手仍發麻。<br><b>參師父：</b>「治心得法。尚有失養者——去究肝之藏血，所養何物。」"],"one":["She thanks you carefully, which is worse than a complaint.<br><b>Master Shen:</b> \"Two lists. Mind and body. You only wrote one of them.\"","她鄭重道謝，比抱怨更難受。<br><b>參師父：</b>「二列：神志與形體。你只寫了一列。」"],"titles":["Su-shi Threads Her Needle","Su-shi Sleeps Through","Su-shi Comes Again Next Week"]},"script":[{"w":"master","en":"This one will not look ill to you. Learn to see shortage — it is quieter than excess.","cn":"此人望之不似有病。當學會看「不足」——其形較有餘為隱。"},{"w":"patient","en":"I had my son a year ago. It was a hard birth, and I lost a lot of blood.","cn":"我一年前生了個兒子。生得艱難，血流了很多。"},{"w":"patient","en":"My heart flutters when I stand up. I sleep, but I dream all night and wake tired.","cn":"起身時心口發慌。睡是睡得著，整夜作夢，醒來還是累。"},{"w":"patient","en":"And my work — I embroider — I cannot see the fine line by evening. My hands go numb holding the frame.","cn":"我做繡活。到了傍晚，細線就看不清了。手撐著繃子會發麻。"},{"w":"master","en":"Flutter, dream, forget — that is one list. Eyes, hands, nails — that is another. Find what joins them.","cn":"悸、夢、忘，此一類也。目、手、爪，又一類也。求其所以相連者。"}],"qa":[{"q":"Tell me about the palpitations — when do they come?","cq":"心悸何時而作？","good":1,"a":"When I stand up quickly, or when I have been sewing a long time. Not when I am angry — just when I am spent.","ca":"急起身時，或繡得久了。不是生氣的時候——是累了的時候。","clue":{"en":"Palpitations on exertion and on standing","zh":"動則心悸，起身尤甚"},"note":"Palpitations that come with exertion and shortage rather than with emotion are deficiency. The Heart is not being filled well enough to keep an even rhythm when demand rises. 因勞因虛而悸，非因情志者，虛也。心失所充，遇動則不能自安其律。"},{"q":"You said you dream all night. Do you remember the dreams?","cq":"言整夜作夢——夢可記否？","good":1,"a":"Every one. Ordinary things, but so many of them that morning feels like a second day.","ca":"都記得。夢的都是尋常事，只是太多，一早醒來像已過了一日。","clue":{"en":"Sleeps but dreams excessively and wakes unrefreshed","zh":"寐而多夢，醒不解乏"},"note":"Blood houses the spirit. With too little blood the spirit floats at night instead of resting in its house — she falls asleep but does not settle, and the night is crowded with dreams. Note the shape: <i>can</i> fall asleep, cannot rest. Empty-heat insomnia is different — she would struggle to fall asleep at all and would wake hot. 血舍神。血不足則神浮於夜而不歸舍：能寐而不安，故夢多。當察其形：能入寐而不得息。虛熱之不寐則異：入寐即難，且醒而覺熱。"},{"q":"Describe what happens to your eyes at the end of the day.","cq":"日暮之時，目何如？","good":1,"a":"Dry first, then the fine line blurs. If I rest them a while it comes back a little.","ca":"先乾，然後細線就糊了。歇一會兒又能看見一點。","clue":{"en":"Dry eyes, blurred vision worse in the evening, better with rest","zh":"目乾視糊，入暮尤甚，得息稍復"},"note":"The Liver stores blood and opens into the eyes; when she rests, blood returns to the Liver and a little vision comes back with it, which is the mechanism demonstrating itself. Compare chapter thirteen: those eyes were dry and gritty <i>with</i> tinnitus and night heat, because that was yin deficiency. Dry eyes alone, with no heat, is blood. 肝藏血而開竅於目。休息則血歸於肝，目稍能視，此機自明。較之第十三章：彼目乾澀而兼耳鳴、夜熱，陰虛故也。但目乾而無熱者，血也。"},{"q":"And the numbness — where, and does anything else happen to the limbs?","cq":"其麻在何處？四肢別有所苦否？","good":1,"a":"Hands and forearms. And my calves cramp at night, hard enough to wake me.","ca":"手和前臂。夜裡小腿還抽筋，抽得能把我痛醒。","clue":{"en":"Numb hands and forearms, night cramps in the calves","zh":"手臂麻木，夜間腓腸拘攣"},"note":"The Liver governs the sinews, and the sinews are moistened by Liver blood. Blood too scant to reach the ends of the limbs gives numbness; sinews too dry to stay soft give cramp. Both are the same shortage arriving at a different tissue. 肝主筋，筋賴肝血以濡。血不榮於四末則麻，筋失其柔則攣。一虧而二見，所至之處異耳。"},{"q":"How have your periods been since the birth?","cq":"產後月事如何？","good":1,"a":"Late, and there is very little. Pale, my mother says, though I do not know how she knows.","ca":"遲，量也很少。我母親說顏色淡——我不知道她怎麼知道的。","clue":{"en":"Scanty, delayed, pale menses","zh":"月經量少色淡而後期"},"note":"Scanty pale delayed menses is blood deficiency stated plainly: there is not enough to fill the vessel on time. In a woman this is often the earliest and most reliable evidence of the pattern, and it is worth asking even when she has not raised it. 經少、色淡、後期，血虛之直言：血不足以按期充其海。婦人之血虛，此證多為最早而最可憑者，雖不自言，亦當問之。"},{"q":"Do you have night sweats, or heat in your palms and soles?","cq":"有盜汗否？手足心熱否？","good":1,"a":"No. I am cold, if anything. My feet are always cold at night.","ca":"沒有。要說的話，是怕冷。夜裡腳總是涼的。","clue":{"en":"No night sweats, no five-centre heat; cold feet","zh":"無盜汗，無五心煩熱；足冷"},"note":"The most valuable question in the chapter, because it decides by <b>absence</b>. Dry eyes, poor sleep and blurred vision would also fit Liver and Kidney yin deficiency — but that pattern brings night sweats, afternoon heat, burning palms, a red peeled tongue and a rapid pulse. She has none of it. Blood deficiency is a shortage of substance without heat; yin deficiency is a shortage of substance <i>with</i> heat. 本章最要之問，以其憑<b>無</b>而決也。目乾、寐差、視糊，肝腎陰虛亦見之；然彼兼盜汗、潮熱、五心煩熱、舌紅無苔、脈數。本案皆無。血虛者，形質不足而無熱；陰虛者，形質不足<b>而有熱</b>。"},{"q":"Is there wandering pain in your ribs?","cq":"脅肋有走竄之痛否？","good":0,"a":"No pain anywhere. I am just... thin, everywhere at once.","ca":"哪裡都不痛。只是⋯⋯到處都空空的。","clue":{"en":"No hypochondriac pain, no distension","zh":"無脅痛，無脹滿"},"note":"Another useful negative. Wandering distending rib pain would mean Liver qi constraint — a full pattern. Her Liver is not stuck; it is empty. The same organ can be too full or too empty, and pain tells you which. 又一有用之陰性所見。脅痛走竄而脹者，肝氣鬱結，實證也。本案之肝非滯而是虛。一臟而或實或虛，痛與不痛可決之。"}],"look":[{"t":"Pale, small and slightly dry, with a thin white coat","c":"舌淡而瘦、稍乾，苔薄白","ok":1,"clue":{"en":"Pale small tongue, thin white coat","zh":"舌淡瘦、苔薄白"},"note":"Correct, and <i>small</i> is doing as much work as <i>pale</i>. Pale says there is not enough blood to bring colour up. Small and thin says there is not enough substance to fill the body of the tongue at all. And a thin white coat says the stomach qi is intact and there is no pathogen — this is a pure shortage, nothing invading. 是，而「瘦」與「淡」同重。淡者血不足以上榮。瘦者形質不足以充其體。苔薄白，則胃氣未敗而無邪——純虛而已，無所侵也。"},{"t":"Pale, swollen and wet, with a white slippery coat","c":"舌淡胖而潤，苔白滑","ok":0,"note":"Pale is right and the rest is not. Swollen and wet is yang deficiency with fluid standing — chapter fifteen. Blood deficiency gives a tongue that is under-filled and slightly dry, not water-logged. Pale-and-thin versus pale-and-fat is a fast way to tell blood from yang. 淡是而餘非。胖而潤者，陽虛水停，第十五章是也。血虛之舌不充而稍乾，非水濕停聚。淡瘦與淡胖之別，辨血虛陽虛之捷徑。"},{"t":"Red with no coating","c":"舌紅無苔","ok":0,"note":"Yin deficiency with empty heat. You are testing the same axis her answer about night sweats already settled — she has no heat anywhere, and she volunteered cold feet. 陰虛有熱之舌。此軸已由其「無盜汗」一答而定：通身無熱，且自言足冷。"},{"t":"Pale with a thick greasy coat","c":"舌淡苔厚膩","ok":0,"note":"Deficiency with damp sitting on top of it. The thick coat would mean something has accumulated, and there is nothing accumulated here — no fullness, no nausea, no heaviness, no loose stool. 虛而兼濕。苔厚膩者必有所積，本案無積：不脹、不嘔、不重、便不溏。"}],"pulse":[{"t":"Thready 細","ok":1,"clue":{"en":"Thready pulse","zh":"脈細"},"note":"Correct, and the reasoning is one step: a thready pulse means the vessel is under-filled, and what fills a vessel is blood and yin. Note what is <i>not</i> here — no rapidity. Thready alone is blood or yin shortage; thready <i>and rapid</i> would add empty heat and change the diagnosis to yin deficiency. One syllable decides it. 是，其推止一步：脈細者脈道不充，而充之者血與陰也。當察其所<b>無</b>——不數。但細者，血虛陰虛之類；細<b>而數</b>，則兼虛熱，其診即為陰虛。一字之差而已。"},{"t":"Thready and rapid 細數","ok":0,"note":"That is the yin deficiency pulse, and the rapid is the whole difference. She has no night sweats, no tidal heat, no red peeled tongue and cold feet — nothing is driving a fast pulse. 此陰虛之脈，所別惟「數」。本案無盜汗、無潮熱、舌不紅而剝、且足冷，無物以驅其數。"},{"t":"Deep and slow 沉遲","ok":0,"note":"Yang deficiency and interior cold. Her feet are cold, which is tempting — but cold extremities in blood deficiency come from blood failing to reach the ends, not from a failure of warmth at the centre. She has no oedema, no clear copious urine, no daybreak diarrhoea. 陽虛裡寒之脈。其足冷可疑，然血虛之厥，血不達四末耳，非中焦失溫。本案無水腫、無小便清長、無五更瀉。"},{"t":"Wiry 弦","ok":0,"note":"Liver constraint or Liver excess. Her Liver is involved but in the opposite way — it is not stuck, it is empty. The pulse is one of the clearest places that distinction shows. 主肝鬱肝實。本案肝誠有病，而其向相反：非滯，乃虛。虛實之判，於脈最見。"}],"dx":[{"ask":"Step 1 — of qi, blood, yin and yang, which is short?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第一步：氣、血、陰、陽，所虧者何？</small>","opts":[{"t":"Blood","c":"血虛","ok":1,"note":"Correct. Take the four in turn. Not <b>qi</b>: qi deficiency is shortness of breath, a weak voice, spontaneous daytime sweating, prolapse — she has none of these and her voice is normal. Not <b>yang</b>: yang deficiency is cold with oedema, clear copious urine, a slow pulse. Not <b>yin</b>: yin deficiency would bring heat, and she has none. <b>Blood</b>: pale tongue, thready pulse, pale lips and nails, dry eyes, numbness, scanty pale periods, palpitations and forgetting — and a documented great loss of blood a year ago. 是。試以四者次第求之。非<b>氣</b>：氣虛者短氣、聲低、自汗、下陷，本案皆無而聲如常。非<b>陽</b>：陽虛則寒而水腫、小便清長、脈遲。非<b>陰</b>：陰虛必見熱，本案無之。<b>血</b>：舌淡、脈細、唇甲淡、目乾、麻木、經少色淡、悸而健忘——且一年前確有大失血。"},{"t":"Qi","c":"氣虛","ok":0,"note":"Qi and blood deficiency travel together often enough that this is a forgivable answer, but read the evidence: her voice is normal, she is not short of breath, she does not sweat by day, nothing has prolapsed. What she reports is the eyes, the sinews, the nails, the periods and the spirit — all things blood feeds. 氣血兩虛，常相因而見，故此答非全謬。然當據所見：聲不低、不短氣、日間不自汗、無下陷。其所苦者，目、筋、爪、經、神——皆血之所養。"},{"t":"Yin","c":"陰虛","ok":0,"note":"The single most common error in this chapter, because dry eyes and poor sleep sound like yin. But yin deficiency is shortage <i>plus heat</i> — night sweats, afternoon tidal heat, burning palms and soles, a red tongue with no coat, a thready <i>rapid</i> pulse. Take away the heat and what remains is blood. 本章最易之誤，以目乾寐差似陰虛故也。然陰虛者，不足<b>而有熱</b>：盜汗、午後潮熱、五心煩熱、舌紅無苔、脈細<b>數</b>。去其熱，則所餘者血耳。"},{"t":"Yang","c":"陽虛","ok":0,"note":"Her feet are cold, and that is the only thing pointing this way. Cold extremities in blood deficiency are blood not reaching the ends; yang deficiency would be cold at the centre too, with oedema, clear urine and a slow pulse. 惟足冷一端似之。血虛之四末不溫者，血不達耳；陽虛則中亦寒，兼水腫、尿清、脈遲。"}]},{"ask":"Step 2 — two organs are named. Give each its own evidence.<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第二步：所名者二臟。當為各臟各立其據。</small>","opts":[{"t":"Heart from the mind, Liver from the body — spirit versus eyes, sinews and nails","c":"心憑神志，肝憑形體 — 神與目筋爪之別","ok":1,"note":"Correct, and this is the comparison the examinations use. <b>Heart blood deficiency</b> shows in the mind, because the Heart houses the spirit: palpitations, insomnia, dream-disturbed sleep, forgetting, being easily startled. <b>Liver blood deficiency</b> shows in the body, because the Liver stores blood and sends it out: dry blurred eyes, numb limbs, tremor, cramp, brittle nails, scanty periods. She has both lists complete. Once you can sort a blood-deficient patient&#39;s symptoms into these two columns, you can name which organ, or both. 是，考試所用之對比正在此。<b>心血虛</b>見於神志，心藏神故：悸、失眠、多夢、健忘、易驚。<b>肝血虛</b>見於形體，肝藏血而輸之故：目乾視糊、肢麻、震顫、拘攣、爪脆、經少。本案二列俱全。能以血虛之症分入此二欄者，則臟之一二可名。"},{"t":"Both organs, but the evidence is the same for each","c":"二臟俱病，而其據無別","ok":0,"note":"Then you could not tell Heart blood deficiency alone from Liver blood deficiency alone, and both exist as separate patterns you will be asked to distinguish. The division is the point. 若然，則單見心血虛與單見肝血虛者無以別，而二者各自成證，考試必令分之。所貴者正在其分。"},{"t":"Heart only — the Liver has no blood deficiency pattern","c":"惟心而已，肝無血虛之證","ok":0,"note":"The opposite of the rule. The empty-cell table is worth memorising: the Liver has <i>no</i> qi deficiency and no yang deficiency pattern, but Liver <b>blood</b> deficiency and Liver <b>yin</b> deficiency are both standard. Spleen and Kidney are the ones with no blood deficiency pattern. 適與其例相反。空格之表宜記：肝<b>無</b>氣虛、<b>無</b>陽虛，而肝血虛、肝陰虛皆為常證。無血虛者，脾與腎也。"},{"t":"Liver only — the palpitations are just anxiety","c":"惟肝而已，心悸不過驚惶","ok":0,"note":"Palpitations, dream-crowded sleep and forgetting are three Heart findings, not a mood. Dismissing them costs you the Heart points and the better half of the formula. 悸、多夢、健忘，心之三證也，非情緒之謂。棄之則失心之穴，且失方之善半。"}]},{"ask":"Step 3 — what is <b>absent</b> here, and why does the absence matter?<br><small class=\"zh\" style=\"color:var(--ink-soft)\">第三步：本案所<b>無</b>者何？其無也，何以為重？</small>","opts":[{"t":"No heat signs — which rules out yin deficiency","c":"無熱之候 — 故非陰虛","ok":1,"note":"Correct, and learning to reason from an absence is the skill this step exists to teach. No night sweats, no afternoon tidal heat, no burning palms and soles, no red peeled tongue, no rapid pulse, no malar flush. Blood deficiency and yin deficiency share nearly every symptom she has — the eyes, the sleep, the dizziness, the thin pulse. Heat is the only reliable line between them, and heat is exactly what is missing. In an examination, when two patterns overlap this much, look for the discriminating absence before you look for another symptom. 是。由「無」而推，正本步所欲授者。無盜汗、無午後潮熱、無五心煩熱、舌不紅而剝、脈不數、無顴紅。血虛與陰虛，本案諸症幾皆共有：目、寐、眩、脈細。所可恃以分者惟熱，而熱正其所無。凡考題二證相疊如此者，先求其別於「所無」，不必更求一症。"},{"t":"No pain — which rules out stagnation","c":"無痛之候 — 故非氣滯","ok":0,"note":"True as far as it goes, and you already established it. But nobody was going to mistake this for Liver qi constraint; the pattern you are actually at risk of confusing it with is yin deficiency, and pain is not what separates those two. 所言不誤，且已明之。然本案本不易誤為肝鬱，所當防者乃陰虛，而二者之別不在痛。"},{"t":"No fever — which rules out an exterior pathogen","c":"無發熱 — 故非外感","ok":0,"note":"A year of symptoms already ruled that out. Absences are only useful when they separate patterns that were genuinely still in contention. 病經一年，早不在表。憑「無」以決者，必其所決之二證猶在爭勝，乃為有用。"},{"t":"Nothing is absent that matters","c":"所無者無足輕重","ok":0,"note":"Then you would treat her for yin deficiency, load her with rich cloying yin tonics she cannot digest, and wonder why her appetite failed and nothing improved. 若然，則以陰虛治之，投滋膩之品而其脾不受，將怪其納減而諸症不減。"}]}],"rx":[{"id":"HT7","t":"Sh&#233;nm&#233;n HT7 神門","ok":1,"note":"Source point of the Heart and the first point for a spirit with nowhere to rest — palpitations, dream-crowded sleep, forgetting, being easily startled. A Source point restores whatever the organ is short of, which here is blood. 心之原穴，神無所舍者首取之：悸、多夢、健忘、易驚。原穴補其臟之所虧，於此所虧者血也。"},{"id":"SP6","t":"S&#257;ny&#299;nji&#257;o SP6 三陰交","ok":1,"note":"Where the Spleen, Liver and Kidney channels cross. It strengthens the Spleen so that blood can be made, and it nourishes Liver blood directly — the point that treats the cause and the consequence with one needle. Remember it is forbidden in pregnancy. 足三陰交會之處。健脾以生血之源，兼養肝血——一針而因果俱及。當記：妊娠禁針。"},{"id":"ST36","t":"Z&#250;s&#257;nl&#464; ST36 足三里","ok":1,"note":"Blood is made from food. Strengthening the Stomach and Spleen is not a detour in a blood deficiency case — it is where new blood comes from, and without it you are moving a small supply around rather than increasing it. 血生於水穀。血虛而健其脾胃，非迂道也，新血之所自出。舍此，則但轉其少許，而非增之。"},{"id":"LR3","t":"T&#224;ich&#333;ng LR3 太衝","ok":0,"note":"Source point of the Liver, and defensible — but in a blood-deficient patient the classical pairing reaches Liver blood through SP6 and the blood-producing points, while LR3 is chiefly the point for moving a stuck or rising Liver. Not wrong so much as second-best, and needled with reduction it would be actively wrong. 肝之原穴，取之非謬。然血虛者，養肝血當由三陰交及生血之穴；太衝所主，在疏其滯、平其亢。其為次選；若以瀉法行之，則實誤矣。"},{"id":"LU6","t":"K&#466;ngzu&#236; LU6 孔最","ok":0,"note":"Yesterday&#39;s point. Cleft points are for acute conditions and bleeding — she is not bleeding now, she bled a year ago, and what remains is the shortage that followed. 昨日之穴。郄穴主急症與血證。本案今不出血，出血在一年之前，所遺者其後之虧耳。"},{"id":"KI7","t":"F&#249;li&#363; KI7 復溜","ok":0,"note":"Warms Kidney yang and moves water. There is no water here and no yang deficiency — her cold feet are blood not reaching the ends, not a failure of warmth at the centre. 溫腎陽而行水。本案無水停，亦無陽虛：其足冷者，血不達四末，非中焦失溫。"}],"tech":[{"t":"Reinforcing method, gentle stimulation, needles retained","c":"補法，輕刺激，留針","ok":1,"note":"<b>Correct on all three counts, and the middle one is a safety matter, not a refinement.</b> Reinforcing, because everything is empty. Gentle, because a blood-deficient patient is the classic candidate for fainting on the table — strong manipulation in someone whose vessels are under-filled can drop her, and this is exactly the patient your clean-needle training warns you about. Retained, because tonifying wants time. Treat her lying down, keep the room warm, and watch her colour. 三者皆是，而中一項為安全所繫，非徒精微。補者，通案皆虛。輕者，血虛之人最易暈針——脈道不充而重手法行之，可令其仆，此正潔針之訓所戒。留針者，補法貴以時。當令其臥而受針，室宜溫，且時察其色。"},{"t":"Reinforcing method with strong stimulation, to get a result","c":"補法，重刺激以求速效","ok":0,"note":"Strong stimulation is not stronger tonification — it is closer to reduction, and in this particular patient it is the recognised set-up for fainting: under-filled vessels, standing or sitting, first treatment, heavy manipulation. If she does go pale and clammy: needles out, lie her flat, raise the legs, keep her warm. 重刺激非補之甚者，其法近瀉。且於此人，暈針之四緣俱備：脈道不充、坐立受針、初診、手法過重。設其面白汗出，則起針、平臥、抬足、保暖。"},{"t":"Reducing method — the sleep is disturbed, so something must be cleared","c":"瀉法 — 寐既不安，必有可清者","ok":0,"note":"Disturbed sleep is not automatically excess. Here the spirit is restless because it has no blood to settle into; taking anything away leaves it with even less. Ask always whether a symptom comes from too much or too little. 寐不安者，未必為實。本案神不安，以無血可舍故；再有所奪，則其舍益薄。凡見一症，必問其為有餘抑或不足。"},{"t":"Reinforcing with generous moxa","c":"補法，重灸","ok":0,"note":"Not a serious error — light moxa is acceptable in blood deficiency without heat, and she does have cold feet. But blood deficiency runs slightly dry, and generous moxa dries further; her tongue was already faintly dry. Moderate warmth, not generous. 其誤不甚——血虛而無熱者，輕灸可也，況足冷。然血虛稍偏於燥，重灸則益燥，其舌本已微乾。當溫而有節，不可過。"}]};
